# Build context exclusions.
#
# The `.env` lines are why this file exists. A build context is uploaded to the
# daemon in full, before any Dockerfile is read, so without these two rules
# `backend/.env` - which holds a real JWT secret and a real master key on every
# developer machine - is sent to the builder and then baked into a layer, where
# `docker history` and any registry it reaches can read it. `**/.env.*` and not
# just `.env` for the same reason .gitignore carries both: `.env.production` is
# the variant most likely to hold real secrets. `.env.example` goes with them and
# nothing in any build stage reads it - a deploy gets the file from version
# control, not from an image.

# Dependencies. Without this the context is two multi-hundred-megabyte
# node_modules trees of native binaries built for the *host* (Windows here, where
# bcrypt's binding is bcrypt_lib.node for win32 and Prisma's is
# query_engine-windows.dll.node). The install inside the image is what produces
# the linux-x64 binaries; copying the host's would build an image that passes
# `COPY` and cannot start.
node_modules
**/node_modules

# Build output. Regenerated by the build stages, and shipping the host's copy
# would defeat the point of building at all - it is how a stale bundle reaches
# production with a fresh timestamp.
dist
**/dist
build
**/build
coverage
**/coverage
*.tsbuildinfo

# VCS and editor state. `.git` alone is tens of megabytes and can carry
# credentials in packed refs or in a hook; neither belongs in an image layer.
.git
.gitignore
.github
.vscode
.idea

# Agent tooling and the local state it leaves behind. None of it is project
# content, and `.opencode/` carries its own node_modules.
.claude
.cursor
.opencode

# Test and runtime artefacts. `test-results/` and `playwright-report/` are
# written by the e2e suite; shipping them adds screenshots of the app's own
# authenticated pages to the image.
test-results
playwright-report
blob-report
.nyc_output
junit.xml
*.lcov

*.log
.DS_Store

# A generated dump of the source tree (imports and comments per file). It goes
# stale on the next edit and is megabytes of content no build stage reads.
project_context.json
